Closures capture by snapshot, not by reference
==============================================

Sysl closures capture their environment **by value at closure-
construction time**. A closure that captures a local `var x`
binds to the *value* `x` had when the closure literal was
evaluated — it does **not** hold a reference to the outer
storage. Two consequences:

  1. Mutating the outer `x` after the closure is built is
     invisible to the closure body — every invocation still
     reads the snapshotted value.

  2. The closure body cannot reach back and mutate the outer
     `x`; it has its own local copy of the captured binding.

This is a deliberate semantic choice — it makes closures pure
under capture and avoids the "spooky action" of Python/JS-style
late binding. The tests below pin both consequences across
all seven backends.

If you genuinely need a shared mutable cell, allocate one
(`new Cell` or similar) and capture the *reference* — that
follows the usual ARC rules and is a different feature.

    module sysl.tests.lang_features

    ccm_apply(f: () -> int) -> int = f()

`val` capture is the simplest case — value frozen forever
---------------------------------------------------------

A `val` cannot be reassigned in the outer scope, so the
captured value never changes regardless of capture semantics.
This is the baseline that every backend must get right.

    #test
    test_val_capture_basic() -> unit
        val k = 10
        val read_k = () -> k
        assert(ccm_apply(read_k) == 10, "val capture reads 10")
        assert(ccm_apply(read_k) == 10, "val capture still reads 10 on repeat")

Snapshot semantics — outer mutation invisible to the closure
------------------------------------------------------------

After the closure literal is evaluated, the outer scope's
`var k` is updated. The closure body still reads the
snapshotted value `10`, not the updated `25`.

    #test
    test_var_capture_is_snapshot() -> unit
        var k = 10
        val read_k = () -> k
        assert(ccm_apply(read_k) == 10, "snapshot at build time = 10")
        k = 25
        assert(ccm_apply(read_k) == 10, "outer mutation invisible — still 10")
        k = -3
        assert(ccm_apply(read_k) == 10, "second outer mutation still invisible")

